## Tips to Optimizing Segmentation Results

### Recommended order for fine-tuning parameters

Start with smaller sections to fine-tune your parameters, as this speeds up computation and allows quicker visualization of results.

{% hint style="info" %}
Be aware that the automated tool **only updates the segmentation within your selected region**, so cells at the edges of the selection box will have **rigid boundaries**.&#x20;

To avoid this issue, you can either select the entire tissue region to ensure smooth segmentation across the tissue, or **use the drawing tools** to fix any misclassified cells while maintaining the accuracy of the rest of the segmentation.
{% endhint %}

1. **Threshold**
   * <mark style="background-color:orange;">Distinguish cell nuclei from the background.</mark>
2. <mark style="background-color:orange;">**Background Radius**</mark>
   * <mark style="background-color:orange;">Controls background estimation, affecting overall signal balance.</mark>
   * <mark style="background-color:orange;">**Sparse Tissue:**</mark> <mark style="background-color:orange;"></mark><mark style="background-color:orange;">Use a</mark> <mark style="background-color:orange;"></mark><mark style="background-color:orange;">**larger**</mark> <mark style="background-color:orange;"></mark><mark style="background-color:orange;">radius for smoother background correction.</mark>
   * <mark style="background-color:orange;">**Dense Tissue:**</mark> <mark style="background-color:orange;"></mark><mark style="background-color:orange;">Use a</mark> <mark style="background-color:orange;"></mark><mark style="background-color:orange;">**smaller**</mark> <mark style="background-color:orange;"></mark><mark style="background-color:orange;">radius to retain more details.</mark>
3. **Sigma (Gaussian Smoothing)**
   * <mark style="background-color:orange;">**Increase**</mark> <mark style="background-color:orange;"></mark><mark style="background-color:orange;">to reduce noise and smooth boundaries.</mark>
   * <mark style="background-color:orange;">**Decrease**</mark> <mark style="background-color:orange;"></mark><mark style="background-color:orange;">to sharpen cell edges and more retain fine details, such as the boundaries between cells.</mark> &#x20;
4. **Median Filter Radius**
   * <mark style="background-color:orange;">**Increase**</mark> <mark style="background-color:orange;"></mark><mark style="background-color:orange;">to remove texture noise or small artifacts, but too high a value may lead to loss of cellular structures.</mark>
5. **Minimum & Maximum Area**
   * <mark style="background-color:orange;">Adjust the cell size range to exclude artifacts or misdetections.</mark>&#x20;

### Different staining types

Generally, **Watershed segmentation algorithm** performs better on **grayscale images** (e.g. ssDNA, DAPI, IF) compared to color images (e.g. H\&E). This is because the algorithm treats the image as a topographic surface, where high intensity denotes peaks and low intensity denotes valleys. In grayscale images, this relationship is straightforward, making segmentation more effective. ([docs.opencv.org](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d3/db4/tutorial_py_watershed.html?utm_source=chatgpt.com))

In contrast, **color images** have multiple channels (e.g. red, green, blue), which can complicate segmentation. To simplify the process, color images are often converted to grayscale before applying the Watershed algorithm.&#x20;

#### For grayscale images

* <mark style="background-color:orange;">**Threshold**</mark><mark style="background-color:orange;">: Set a</mark> <mark style="background-color:orange;"></mark><mark style="background-color:orange;">**higher**</mark> <mark style="background-color:orange;"></mark><mark style="background-color:orange;">intensity threshold to separate cell nuclei from the background effectively.</mark>
* <mark style="background-color:orange;">**Minimum/Maximum Area**</mark><mark style="background-color:orange;">: Adjust based on the expected cell size to filter out noise and artifacts.</mark>

#### For color images

* <mark style="background-color:orange;">**Sigma**</mark><mark style="background-color:orange;">:</mark> <mark style="background-color:orange;"></mark><mark style="background-color:orange;">**Increase**</mark> <mark style="background-color:orange;"></mark><mark style="background-color:orange;">to reduce noise and smooth image boundaries.</mark>&#x20;
* <mark style="background-color:orange;">**Median Filter Radius**</mark><mark style="background-color:orange;">: Adjust to remove texture-related noise and enhance segmentation accuracy.</mark>

### Dense vs. sparse cell regions

#### For dense cell regions:

* <mark style="background-color:orange;">**Background Radius**</mark><mark style="background-color:orange;">: Use a</mark> <mark style="background-color:orange;"></mark><mark style="background-color:orange;">**smaller**</mark> <mark style="background-color:orange;"></mark><mark style="background-color:orange;">background radius to preserve fine details.</mark>
* <mark style="background-color:orange;">**Maximum Area**</mark><mark style="background-color:orange;">: Lower the value to prevent cell merging.</mark>

#### For sparse cell regions:

* <mark style="background-color:orange;">**Background Radius**</mark><mark style="background-color:orange;">: Use a</mark> <mark style="background-color:orange;"></mark><mark style="background-color:orange;">**larger**</mark> <mark style="background-color:orange;"></mark><mark style="background-color:orange;">background radius to effectively subtrack large-scale background variantions.</mark>
* <mark style="background-color:orange;">**Minimum Area**</mark><mark style="background-color:orange;">: Increase cell size to reduce background noise misidentification.</mark>

## **Troubleshooting & Frequently Asked Questions**

### Q: Why the default parameter settings changed for different image?

A: For **Automated segmentation - Watershed**, the pixel size of a image is a key characteristic which determines how physical dimensions are represented in the image, and therefore, affect the recommend value of other parameters.&#x20;
